  "task_completions": [
    "The last time the U.S. federal government had a budget surplus was in **Fiscal Year 2000**. \n\nHere's a bit more detail:\n\n*   **FY2000 Surplus:** $236.9 billion\n*   This was during the Clinton administration, a period of economic growth and fiscal discipline.\n*   Since then, the U.S. has consistently run budget deficits, meaning it spends more than it takes in through revenue.\n\n\n\nYou can find more information from these sources:\n\n*   **Congressional Budget Office (CBO):** [https://www.cbo.gov/topics/budget](https://www.cbo.gov/topics/budget)\n*   **U.S. Office of Management and Budget (OMB):** [https://www.whitehouse.gov/omb/budget/](https://www.whitehouse.gov/omb/budget/)\n*   **The Balance:** [https://www.thebalancemoney.com/us-budget-surplus-deficit-history-3306347](https://www.thebalancemoney.com/us-budget-surplus-deficit-history-3306347)",
    "The last time the US federal government had a budget surplus was in **Fiscal Year 2000**. \n\nSpecifically, it was a surplus of **$236.98 billion**.\n\nHere's a little more context:\n\n*   The US actually had a string of surpluses in the late 1990s/early 2000s (FY1998, FY1999, and FY2000).\n*   These surpluses were largely due to a combination of factors: strong economic growth during the dot-com boom, spending restraint, and tax increases from earlier legislation.\n*   Since 2001, the US has consistently run budget deficits.\n\n\n\nYou can find more information from these sources:\n\n*   **Congressional Budget Office (CBO):** [https://www.cbo.gov/topics/budget](https://www.cbo.gov/topics/budget)\n*   **U.S. Department of the Treasury:** [https://home.treasury.gov/resource-center/data-chart-center/interest-rates/historical-budget-surplus-or-deficit](https://home.treasury.gov/resource-center/data-chart-center/interest-rates/historical-budget-surplus-or-deficit)\n*   **The Balance:** [https://www.thebalancemoney.com/us-budget-surplus-deficit-history-3306008](https://www.thebalancemoney.com/us-budget-surplus-deficit-history-3306008)",
